Issue: The handheld plasma cutter leaves irregular cuts due to human error

Goals: 
    1. Guide the plasma cutter along a straight path to cut clean and straight lines
    2. The plasma cutter needs to be able to travel forward and backwards along the given line
    3. Feeds and speeds need to be able to be adjusted simply and manually so that different materials can be cut at different speeds dependent on thickness

Concept:
    1. Use a supported linear rod and a threaded rod, spun by a stepper motor, to drive a platform with a mount for the plasma torch
    2. The stepper motor needs to be detachable (for transportation) and should be powered by a standard 120VAC outlet

Issues Encountered:
    1. The original stepper used (standard NEMA 17) did not have a high enough holding torque to have a rigid assembly
        A. Solution: Replaced NEMA 17 with NEMA 23 and an external driver

Final Design:
    1. The external box contains the power supply, driver, power switch, and driver controller, with cords to the motor and outlet
    2. Mounting bracket and thread to D-Shaft adapter modeled in Fusion 360 and printed in PETG on a Bambu X1 Carbon that slides on the rail to support the motor and connect the motor to the threaded rod
    3. Direction and RPM can be controlled on the driver controller to vary feeds and speeds
